# Kanji Stroke Order Diagram

Fetches open-source KanjiVG data and renders a progressive stroke-by-stroke diagram as an HTML artifact.

## Step 1 — Codepoint

Convert the target character to a zero-padded 5-digit lowercase hex.
Example: 字 → U+5B57 → `05b57`

```python
char = "字"
cp = f"{ord(char):05x}"  # → "05b57"
```

## Step 2 — Fetch KanjiVG

```
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/KanjiVG/kanjivg/master/kanji/{cp}.svg
```

Use bash or Python `urllib`. If 404 → character has no KanjiVG entry; output error and suggest the base form.

Parse strokes with a single regex (no XML parser needed):

```python
import re, urllib.request

url = f"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/KanjiVG/kanjivg/master/kanji/{cp}.svg"
svg = urllib.request.urlopen(url).read().decode()

# Strokes in document order = stroke order
strokes = re.findall(r'<path id="kvg:[^-]+-s\d+"[^>]*d="([^"]+)"', svg)
# → list of SVG path d-strings, index 0 = stroke 1
```

## Step 3 — Render HTML artifact

Produce one panel per stroke. Panel i shows:
- Strokes 1…i−1 in `#c8c8c8` (thin, 2.5px) — already drawn
- Stroke i in `#111111` (bold, 3.5px) — current stroke
- A numbered start-dot (filled circle, r=5) at the stroke's first M coordinate
- A small arrowhead at the stroke's last coordinate, approximating direction from the second-to-last coordinate

Extract first point from first `M` or `m` command; last point from the final coordinate pair in the path.

```python
def first_point(d):
    m = re.search(r'[Mm]\s*([\d.]+)[,\s]+([\d.]+)', d)
    return float(m.group(1)), float(m.group(2))

def last_point(d):
    nums = re.findall(r'-?[\d]+\.?\d*', d)
    return float(nums[-2]), float(nums[-1])

def second_to_last_point(d):
    nums = re.findall(r'-?[\d]+\.?\d*', d)
    if len(nums) < 4: return last_point(d)
    return float(nums[-4]), float(nums[-3])
```

Arrow rendering — draw a small filled triangle at the endpoint:

```python
import math

def arrow_svg(x2, y2, x1, y1, size=5):
    angle = math.atan2(y2 - y1, x2 - x1)
    a1 = angle + 2.5
    a2 = angle - 2.5
    pts = f"{x2},{y2} {x2+size*math.cos(a1)},{y2+size*math.sin(a1)} {x2+size*math.cos(a2)},{y2+size*math.sin(a2)}"
    return f'<polygon points="{pts}" fill="#111"/>'
```

## Output layout

```html
<div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px;padding:16px;background:#faf9f7;font-family:sans-serif">
  <!-- N panels, each: -->
  <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:4px">
    <svg width="90" height="90" viewBox="0 0 109 109">
      <!-- gray strokes 1..i-1 -->
      <!-- black stroke i -->
      <!-- start dot + number -->
      <!-- end arrowhead -->
    </svg>
    <span style="font-size:11px;color:#888">{i}</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

Panel count ≤ 30 strokes; wrap naturally.

## Frugal execution path

Run everything in one `bash_tool` Python call: fetch → parse → generate HTML → write to `/mnt/user-data/outputs/stroke-{char}.html` → call `present_files`.

Keep the script under 60 lines. No external deps beyond stdlib.

